window.MODULE_SLIDES = {"1": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m1/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 1", "text": "MODULE 1\nCodeIgniter Foundations", "image": "slides/m1/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nExplain what a web application framework is and why \ndevelopers use one\n•\nDescribe how the Model-View-Controller (MVC) pattern \nseparates data, logic, and presentation\n•\nInstall CodeIgniter 4 with Composer and recognize its \nfolder structure\n•\nCreate routes, a controller, and a view — and pass \ndata between them", "image": "slides/m1/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nWeb Application \nFrameworks and MVC\n• What a framework is and \nwhy we use one\n• The MVC pattern: Model, \nView, Controller\n• How CodeIgniter fits in\nSubtopic 2\nCodeIgniter Setup and Basic \nPages\n•\nInstalling CodeIgniter 4 with \nComposer\n•\nYour first route, controller, and \nview\n•\nPassing data from controller to \nview", "image": "slides/m1/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nWeb Application Frameworks and MVC", "image": "slides/m1/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "What Is a Web Application?", "text": "What Is a Web Application?\nA web application is a program that runs on a server and is \nused through a web browser — no installation on the user’s \ndevice required.\nEvery visit follows the same basic pattern:\n•\nThe browser asks for a page (a request)\n•\nThe server figures out what to send back\n•\nThe server replies with HTML the browser can display \n(a response)\nThe systems we’ll build all live on the server side of that exchange.", "image": "slides/m1/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "The Request-Response Cycle", "text": "The Request-Response Cycle\nEvery page load is a conversation \nbetween two computers.\nThe browser never talks to your \nPHP code directly — it only ever \nsends a request and waits for a \nresponse.\nBrowser (Client)\n1. Request  ↓\n↑  2. Response\nServer", "image": "slides/m1/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Why Use a Web Framework?", "text": "Why Use a Web Framework?\nWriting every page in plain PHP means solving the same \nproblems over and over: routing URLs, talking to the \ndatabase safely, checking user input, organizing files.\nA framework is a pre-built structure and toolkit that handles \nthe repetitive, easy-to-get-wrong parts, so you focus on what \nmakes your application unique.\nThat’s the trade a framework offers: a little bit of \nstructure to learn, in exchange for a lot of repeated work \nyou never have to do again.", "image": "slides/m1/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "Without vs. With a Framework", "text": "Without vs. With a Framework\nPlain PHP, page by page\n•\nOne .php file per URL, each \nwith its own copy-pasted setup\n•\nDatabase code, validation, and \nHTML mixed together\n•\nSecurity checks are easy to \nforget on any given page\n•\nNo shared convention for \nwhere anything lives\nWith a Framework\n•\nURLs are mapped to code in \none place: the routes file\n•\nData, logic, and display are \nkept in separate, predictable \nfiles\n•\nCommon security and \nvalidation tools are built in\n•\nEvery CodeIgniter project \nshares the same folder layout", "image": "slides/m1/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "What Is MVC?", "text": "What Is MVC?\nModel-View-Controller (MVC) is a way of organizing an \napplication by splitting it into three areas of responsibility.\nInstead of one file that fetches data, applies logic, and prints \nHTML all at once, MVC keeps those three jobs in three \ndifferent kinds of files.\nMost frameworks are built around MVC, so almost \neverything you write will live in one of these three \nplaces.", "image": "slides/m1/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "The Three Roles at a Glance", "text": "The Three Roles at a Glance\nMVC splits an application into \nthree cooperating parts.\nEach one has exactly one job, so \na change to one rarely breaks the \nothers.\nModel\nData & business rules\nView\nWhat the user sees\nController\nHandles the request", "image": "slides/m1/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "Model", "text": "Model\nThe Model manages your application’s data.\n•\nTalks to the database — retrieving, inserting, \nupdating, and deleting records\n•\nHolds the business rules for that data (for example, \nwhat counts as a valid price)\n•\nKnows nothing about HTML or how the data will be \ndisplayed\nIn CodeIgniter, a Model is a PHP class in app/Models", "image": "slides/m1/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "View", "text": "View\nThe View is what the user actually sees.\n•\nAn HTML template, usually with small bits of PHP to \ndisplay data\n•\nFocused purely on presentation — layout, text, styling\n•\nDoes not fetch data or make decisions; it just displays \nwhat it’s given\n•\nThe user interface of the web application\nIn CodeIgniter, a View is a .php file in app/Views", "image": "slides/m1/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Controller", "text": "Controller\nThe Controller is the coordinator.\n•\nReceives the incoming request and decides what \nshould happen\n•\nAsks the Model for any data it needs\n•\nSends that data to a View and returns the finished \nresponse\nIn CodeIgniter, a Controller is a PHP class in app/Controllers", "image": "slides/m1/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "How a Request Flows Through MVC", "text": "How a Request Flows Through MVC\nBrowser\nController\n2. Routes here\nModel\n3. Asks for data and returns \ndata to Controllers\nView\n4. Gets rendered\nBrowser\n5. Sees the page\nThe Controller is the only one that talks to both the Model and the View — they never talk to each other directly.\n1. Send request", "image": "slides/m1/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "Why This Separation Matters", "text": "Why This Separation Matters\n•\nEasier to maintain — fixing a display issue means \nediting a View, not hunting through database code\n•\nEasier to work in a team — one developer can build \nthe View while another builds the Model\n•\nEasier to test — you can check the Model’s logic \nwithout needing a browser at all\n•\nEasier to reuse — the same Model can feed a web \npage, a mobile app, or an API", "image": "slides/m1/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "Introducing CodeIgniter", "text": "Introducing CodeIgniter\nCodeIgniter is a free, open-source PHP framework built around \nthe MVC pattern.\n•\nLightweight and fast, with a small learning curve for \nbeginners\n•\nWell-documented, with an official user guide for every \nfeature\n•\nWidely used in the Philippine IT industry for business web \nsystems\n•\nCurrently on version 4 (CodeIgniter 4), a full rewrite of the \nolder CodeIgniter 3", "image": "slides/m1/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "MVC in one sentence: the Model holds the data,", "text": "MVC in one sentence: the Model holds the data, \nthe View displays it, and the Controller decides \nwhat happens in between.", "image": "slides/m1/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nCodeIgniter Setup and Basic Pages", "image": "slides/m1/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "Before You Install: What You Need", "text": "Before You Install: What You Need\nA few tools should already be on your machine before you start:\n•\nPHP 8.1 or newer — CodeIgniter 4 is a PHP framework, so \nPHP itself has to be installed\n•\nComposer — the PHP package manager we’ll use to install \nCodeIgniter (optional if you’ll download the framework files \ndirectly)\n•\nA local server stack such as XAMPP — gives you PHP and \nMySQL together\n•\nA code editor such as VS Code", "image": "slides/m1/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Installing Composer", "text": "Installing Composer\nComposer downloads and \nmanages the packages that \nmake up a CodeIgniter project.\nYou only install it once per \nmachine, then reuse it for every \nPHP project you build.\nGet it from getcomposer.org, \nthen confirm the install:\n$ composer --version\nComposer version 2.9.1 2026-01-14 \n09:12:26", "image": "slides/m1/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Creating a New CodeIgniter Project", "text": "Creating a New CodeIgniter Project\nOne Composer command \ndownloads CodeIgniter and sets up \na ready-to-run project folder.\nRun this command from the folder \nwhere you keep your projects:\ncomposer create-project \ncodeigniter4/appstarter \nmyproject\n$ composer create-project \ncodeigniter4/appstarter myproject\nCreating a \"codeigniter4/appstarter\" \nproject\nInstalling codeigniter4/framework...\nGenerated optimized autoload files", "image": "slides/m1/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Creating New Project (alternative)", "text": "Creating New Project (alternative)\nAlternatively, you can also \ndownload the CodeIgniter files \n(zip) from their official \nwebsite:\nhttps://www.codeigniter.com/d\nownload", "image": "slides/m1/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "Project Structure at a Glance", "text": "Project Structure at a Glance\nComposer generates a full folder \nstructure for you.\nYou’ll spend most of your time in \njust a few of these folders.\nNote that the vendor folder is replaced with system if you \nmanually downloaded CodeIgniter.\nmyproject/\n├── app/\n│   ├── Controllers/\n│   ├── Models/\n│   ├── Views/\n│   └── Config/\n├── public/      ← document root\n├── writable/    ← logs & cache\n├── vendor/      ← Composer packages\n└── .env         ← local settings", "image": "slides/m1/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Key Folders You'll Use Most", "text": "Key Folders You'll Use Most\napp/Controllers — the classes that receive \nrequests and decide what to do\napp/Models — classes that talk to the database\napp/Views — the HTML/PHP templates users see in \ntheir web browsers\napp/Config — settings, including Routes.php", "image": "slides/m1/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "The Public Folder and .htaccess", "text": "The Public Folder and .htaccess\npublic/ is the only folder your web server should point to —\nit’s the document root.\nEverything else (app/, writable/, vendor/) stays outside the \npublic folder, so it can never be accessed directly from a \nbrowser URL.\npublic/.htaccess quietly rewrites every URL through \nindex.php, which is what lets your routes work without it \nshowing in the address bar.", "image": "slides/m1/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Running the Development Server", "text": "Running the Development Server\nCodeIgniter ships with Spark, a \ncommand-line tool. Its serve \ncommand starts a quick local \nserver — no separate \nApache/XAMPP setup needed \nwhile you develop.\nRun this from inside your project \nfolder:\n$ php spark serve\nCodeIgniter development server \nstarted\nListening on \nhttp://localhost:8080\nPress Control-C to stop.", "image": "slides/m1/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Running Apache (XAMPP)", "text": "Running Apache (XAMPP)\nAlternatively, you can also use \nApache server via the XAMPP \napplication. Ensure that the \nApache service is currently \nrunning (click Start button if \nneeded)", "image": "slides/m1/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Your First Look at CodeIgniter", "text": "Your First Look at CodeIgniter\nVisit the address the server gave \nyou (usually http://localhost:8080 if \nPHP spark is used or \nlocalhost/your-folder-name/public\nif you are using XAMPP), and \nCodeIgniter’s default welcome page \nconfirms the install worked — before \nyou’ve written a single line of your \nown code.\nlocalhost:8080\nWelcome to \nCodeIgniter 4!\nIf you see this, your project \nwas set up correctly.", "image": "slides/m1/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Initial Configurations", "text": "Initial Configurations\nConfiguring the environment: \nCodeIgniter 4 requires some initial configuration \nto work correctly. Follow these:\n1.\nRename the env file (located in the root \ndirectory) to .env. This file contains \nenvironment-specific settings.\n2.\nOpen the .env file in a text editor.\n3.\nSet the CI_ENVIRONMENT variable to \ndevelopment or production, depending on \nyour needs.\n4.\nSet the app.baseURL to your domain name \n(typically: http://localhost/folder-name/ if you \nare still in the development.)\n#-------------------------------------------\n# ENVIRONMENT\n#-------------------------------------------\nCI_ENVIRONMENT = development\n#-------------------------------------------\n# APP\n#-------------------------------------------\napp.baseURL = 'http://localhost/folder-name/", "image": "slides/m1/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Initial Configurations", "text": "Initial Configurations\nThe base URL configuration tells CodeIgniter where the \napplication is located.\nOpen app/Config/App.php in a text editor. \nLocate the $baseURL variable and set it to the URL where \nyour application will be accessed.\nIf you need more flexibility, the baseURL may be set within the .env file as app.baseURL = 'http://localhost/your-\nproject-folder/'.\nAlways use a trailing slash on your base URL!\napp/Config/App.php\npublic $baseURL = 'http://localhost/your-folder-name/';", "image": "slides/m1/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Initial Configurations", "text": "Initial Configurations\nThen, in the app/Config/App.php file, locate the line with the \nvariable $indexPage = 'index.php' and change it to:\napp/Config/App.php\npublic $indexPage = '';", "image": "slides/m1/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "When referring to CodeIgniter URLs, we use the following", "text": "When referring to CodeIgniter URLs, we use the following \nterms:\nExample: https://www.example.com/blog/news/2022/10?page=2\nUnderstanding the URL\nTerm\nExample\nDescription\nBase URL\nhttps://www.example.com/\nor\nhttps://www.example.com/blog/\n- if the base URL contains subfolder\nBase URL is often denoted as baseURL when \nused in your codes.\nURI path\n/blog/news/2022/10\nThis is used in the Routes.php config file for \nrouting.\nRoute path\n/blog/news/2022/10\nThe URI path relative to the Base URL. It is \nalso called as URI string.\nQuery\npage=2\nUsually used when using GET method in \nforms.", "image": "slides/m1/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "What Is Routing?", "text": "What Is Routing?\nRouting is how CodeIgniter decides which code should run \nfor a given URL.\nEvery route lives in one file, \napp/Config/Routes.php\nCodeIgniter checks the routes you’ve defined, top to bottom, \nuntil it finds one that matches the requested URL.", "image": "slides/m1/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "title": "Anatomy of a Route", "text": "Anatomy of a Route\nA defined route has three parts: the HTTP method, the URL \npattern, and the controller action to run.\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/about', 'Pages::about');\nget — the HTTP method, responds only to GET requests (a normal page visit)\n'/about' — the URL pattern to match, after your domain (Example: \nhttps://yourdomain.com/about)\n'Pages::about' — run the about() method on the Pages controller", "image": "slides/m1/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "title": "CodeIgniter URLs are segment-based. For example, for", "text": "CodeIgniter URLs are segment-based. For example, for \nthe URL: https://domain-name/blog/post/20261231\nUnderstanding URI\nPart\nExample\nDescription\nBase URL\nhttps://domain-name/\nThis is the base URL of your \nCodeIgniter 4 application\nSegment 1\nblog\nThis is the name of the Controller\nSegment 2\npost\nThis is the name of the function in \nthe Blog controller (segment 1).\nSegment 3 … n\n20261231\nThis is the parameter value that will \nbe passed to the function (segment \n2).", "image": "slides/m1/036.webp"}, {"page": 37, "title": "If a function to be called in a route needs parameters,", "text": "If a function to be called in a route needs parameters, \nplaceholders are needed. For example, for the URL:\nhttps://your-domain/profile/2026123\nYou will need to write the route as:\nRoute Path Placeholders\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/profile/(:num)', 'Pages::profile/$1');", "image": "slides/m1/037.webp"}, {"page": 38, "title": "Here are the different placeholders that can be used in", "text": "Here are the different placeholders that can be used in \nthe routes:\nRoute Path Placeholders\nPlaceholders\nDescription\n(:any)\nwill match all characters from that point to the end of the URI. This may include \nmultiple URI segments.\n(:segment)\nwill match any character except for a forward slash (/) restricting the result to a \nsingle segment.\n(:num)\nwill match any positive integer.\n(:alpha)\nwill match any string of alphabetic characters\n(:alphanum)\nwill match any string of alphabetic characters or integers, or any combination of \nthe two.\n(:hash)\nis the same as (:segment), but can be used to easily see which routes use hashed ids.", "image": "slides/m1/038.webp"}, {"page": 39, "title": "What Is a Controller?", "text": "What Is a Controller?\nA controller is a PHP class — one class is created per group \nof related pages.\n•\nEach public method inside it is an action that a route \ncan point to.\n•\nEvery controller extends CodeIgniter’s BaseController, \nwhich gives it helpful built-in tools.\n•\nController files are stored in app/Controllers folder \nand follow the same name as the class.", "image": "slides/m1/039.webp"}, {"page": 40, "title": "Writing Your First Controller", "text": "Writing Your First Controller\nCreate app/Controllers/Pages.php:\napp/Controllers/Pages.php\n<?php\nnamespace App\\Controllers;\nclass Pages extends BaseController\n{\npublic function about()\n{\nreturn 'This is the about page.';\n}\n}", "image": "slides/m1/040.webp"}, {"page": 41, "title": "Connecting the Route and Testing It", "text": "Connecting the Route and Testing It\nWith the controller in place, the \nroute from the earlier slide now \nhas somewhere to send the \nrequest:\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/about', 'Pages::about');\nlocalhost:8080/about\nThis is the about page.", "image": "slides/m1/041.webp"}, {"page": 42, "title": "The index function", "text": "The index function\nThe index function of a Controller is always the first function \nto execute when calling a Controller from a route:\napp/Controllers/Pages.php\n<?php\nnamespace App\\Controllers;\nclass Pages extends BaseController\n{\npublic function index()\n{\nreturn 'This is an index page.';\n}\n}\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('', 'Pages::index');\nor\n$routes->get('/', 'Pages::index');", "image": "slides/m1/042.webp"}, {"page": 43, "title": "By default, URLs of a CodeIgniter project looks like this:", "text": "By default, URLs of a CodeIgniter project looks like this: \nhttp://domain-name/public/index.php/home\nThis is because all access must start from the public \nfolder.\nYou have to clean this URL by removing \npublic/index.php/ from it.\nCleaning the URL", "image": "slides/m1/043.webp"}, {"page": 44, "title": "There are two ways to clean the URL.", "text": "There are two ways to clean the URL.\nCleaning the URL\nMove index.php and .htaccess\n1. Open the public folder and find the \nfiles  index.php and .htaccess\n2. Move them out of the public folder \nand put them inside your root \nfolder\n3. Open the moved index.php file, \nremove the ../ from the line\nThis method is not recommended for applications that \nwill be hosted.\nCreate a new .htaccess file\n1. Create a new file inside the root \ndirectory and name it .htaccess\n2. Put the codes below in the created \nfile:\nThis method is safer for hosted CodeIgniter projects.\n<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>\nRewriteEngine On\nRewriteRule ^(.*)$ public/$1 [L]\n</IfModule>\nrequire FCPATH . 'app/Config/Paths.php';", "image": "slides/m1/044.webp"}, {"page": 45, "title": "What Is a View?", "text": "What Is a View?\nReturning plain text from a controller works, but real pages \nneed HTML — that’s what a View is for.\n•\nA View is a .php file that mixes HTML with small bits of \nPHP to display data.\n•\nView files are stored in app/Views folder, and a \ncontroller loads one with the view() function.\n•\nKeeping HTML in Views — instead of inside the \ncontroller — keeps each file focused on one job.", "image": "slides/m1/045.webp"}, {"page": 46, "title": "Creating Your First View", "text": "Creating Your First View\nCreate app/Views/about.php:\napp/Views/about.php\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<title>About</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h1>This is the about page.</h1>\n</body>\n</html>", "image": "slides/m1/046.webp"}, {"page": 47, "title": "Loading the View from the Controller", "text": "Loading the View from the Controller\nChange the plain-text return \nstatement from earlier for a call \nto view():\napp/Controllers/Pages.php\npublic function about()\n{\nreturn view('about');\n}\nlocalhost:8080/about\nThis is the about page.", "image": "slides/m1/047.webp"}, {"page": 48, "title": "Passing Data to Views", "text": "Passing Data to Views\nA view() call takes an optional second argument: an array of \ndata for the view to use.\n•\nEach array key becomes a variable the View can print \ndirectly — no extra wiring needed.\n•\nThis is how the Controller hands off data without the \nView needing to know where it came from.", "image": "slides/m1/048.webp"}, {"page": 49, "title": "Example: Passing Dynamic Data", "text": "Example: Passing Dynamic Data\nPages.php\npublic function about()\n{\n$data = [\n'name' => 'Pages',\n];\nreturn view(\n'about', $data\n);\n}\nabout.php\n<h1>\nAbout\n<?= $name ?>\n</h1>\nThe array key 'name' automatically becomes the $name variable the view can print directly.", "image": "slides/m1/049.webp"}, {"page": 50, "title": "Loading Multiple Views", "text": "Loading Multiple Views\nCodeIgniter can return several \nview files as one response. \nConcatenate the view() calls so \nthe controller returns one \ncomplete page.\napp/Controllers/Pages.php\npublic function about()\n{\n$data = [\n'title' => 'Sample Page',\n'name'\n=> 'Pages',\n];\nreturn view('header', $data)\n. view('menu')\n. view('main', $data)\n. view('footer');\n}", "image": "slides/m1/050.webp"}, {"page": 51, "title": "Your view files can also be stored within sub-directories if you", "text": "Your view files can also be stored within sub-directories if you \nprefer that type of organization. When doing so you will need \nto include the directory name when loading the view. \nExample:\nOrganizing You View Files\napp/Controllers/Pages.php\npublic function about()\n{\nreturn view('directory-name/about');\n}", "image": "slides/m1/051.webp"}, {"page": 52, "title": "Putting It All Together", "text": "Putting It All Together\nRoutes.php\n/about\nPages.php\nabout()\nabout.php\nthe View\nBrowser\nrenders HTML\nA route maps a URL to a controller method, the method gathers data and hands it to \na view, and the view renders the page the browser displays.", "image": "slides/m1/052.webp"}, {"page": 53, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nForgetting the namespace line in a \ncontroller\n•\nView filename not matching the \nstring in view()\n•\nEditing a file, but the browser still \nshows old output — do a hard \nrefresh\n•\nA route defined for get() being \nvisited in a way that doesn’t match\nRemember\n•\nA framework removes repetitive \nwork so you focus on your app\n•\nMVC: Model = data, View = display, \nController = coordinator\n•\nRoutes.php connects a URL to a \ncontroller method\n•\nview() renders a template, \noptionally with data", "image": "slides/m1/053.webp"}, {"page": 54, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about frameworks, MVC, or \nyour first CodeIgniter pages?", "image": "slides/m1/054.webp"}], "2": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m2/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 2", "text": "MODULE 2\nCodeIgniter Data Layer", "image": "slides/m2/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nConfigure a CodeIgniter project to connect to a \nMySQL database\n•\nExplain what a Model is and create one for a database \ntable\n•\nUse Query Builder to retrieve, filter, and sort records\n•\nPass database records from a controller to a view and \ndisplay them", "image": "slides/m2/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nDatabase Configuration\n•\nCreating a database and table\n•\nConnecting CodeIgniter to the \ndatabase\n•\nTesting the connection\nSubtopic 2\nModels and Query Builder\n•\nCreating a Model for a table\n•\nRetrieving, filtering, and \nsorting records\n•\nDisplaying records in a view", "image": "slides/m2/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nDatabase Configuration", "image": "slides/m2/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "Why Applications Need a Database", "text": "Why Applications Need a Database\nSimple pages can display fixed text or arrays, but those \nvalues are not real records.\nA database stores records that survive restarts, can be \nshared across requests, and can grow safely.\nFrom this point, the examples use a small TODO tasks table so the Model -> \nController -> View flow stays consistent.", "image": "slides/m2/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Creating the Database and Table", "text": "Creating the Database and Table\nOpen phpMyAdmin from \nXAMPP’s control panel and \ncreate a new database.\nThen run this SQL to create our \nfirst table:\nCREATE TABLE tasks (\nid INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\ntitle VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL,\nstatus VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT\n'pending',\ncreated_at DATETIME\n);", "image": "slides/m2/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "The tasks Table", "text": "The tasks Table\nThis is the table our example app \nwill build on for the rest of the \ncourse.\nEvery column has a name and a \ndata type — CodeIgniter will read \nthis structure automatically.\ntasks\nColumn       Type\n──────────   ─────────────────\nid           INT (auto)\ntitle        VARCHAR(150)\nstatus       VARCHAR(20)\ncreated_at   DATETIME", "image": "slides/m2/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "Add Sample Rows Before Reading", "text": "Add Sample Rows Before Reading\nAfter CREATE TABLE, the table \nexists but has no records yet.\nWe will be using these sample data in \nthe next slides.\nINSERT INTO tasks (title, status, \ncreated_at) VALUES\n('Buy groceries', 'pending', NOW()),\n('Submit report', 'pending', NOW()),\n('Fix login bug', 'done', NOW());", "image": "slides/m2/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "Configuring Database Settings in .env", "text": "Configuring Database Settings in .env\nYou can connect CodeIgniter to the database using the .env \nfile. Open the .env file in your project root and find the \nfollowing lines and modify them as follows:\n.env\ndatabase.default.hostname = localhost\ndatabase.default.database = your_database_name\ndatabase.default.username = root\ndatabase.default.password =\ndatabase.default.DBDriver = MySQLi", "image": "slides/m2/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "Although using the .env file is enough, you might also", "text": "Although using the .env file is enough, you might also \nwant to set the same in the Database.php file.\nConfiguring Database Settings in\napp/Config/Database.php\nApp/Config/Database.php\n'hostname'     => 'localhost',\n'username'     => 'root',\n'password'     => '',\n'database'     => 'your_database_name',\n'DBDriver'     => 'MySQLi',", "image": "slides/m2/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "How Config/Database.php Uses These Settings", "text": "How Config/Database.php Uses These Settings\napp/Config/Database.php defines the default values \nCodeIgniter falls back on.\nAny matching key in .env automatically overrides the value in \nthis file — you rarely need to edit Database.php directly.\nThis is the same pattern you already saw for app.baseURL in Module 1 — .env always wins.", "image": "slides/m2/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "Why Not Hardcode Credentials?", "text": "Why Not Hardcode Credentials?\nIt’s tempting to type your database username and \npassword directly into a config file (i.e. Database.php) —\nbut that’s risky.\n•\nHardcoded credentials get committed to version control \nand shared by accident\n•\nEvery environment (your laptop, a classmate’s, the live \nserver) needs different values\n•\nCodeIgniter solves this with a .env file: one set of local \nsettings, never shared or uploaded to a version control.", "image": "slides/m2/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Testing the Connection", "text": "Testing the Connection\nStart the server and visit any \npage.\nIf your settings are correct, the \npage loads normally. If not, \nCodeIgniter shows a clear \ndatabase error — that’s your cue \nto recheck .env.\n$ php spark serve\nCodeIgniter development server started\nListening on http://localhost:8080", "image": "slides/m2/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "Common Connection Errors to Watch For", "text": "Common Connection Errors to Watch For\n•\nUnknown database — the database name in .env (or \nDatabase.php) doesn’t exist yet in phpMyAdmin\n•\nAccess denied for user — wrong username or \npassword\n•\nConnection refused — MySQL isn’t running in \nXAMPP’s control panel\n•\nSettings not taking effect — a stray space or missing \nkey name in .env", "image": "slides/m2/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": ".env holds where and how to connect;", "text": ".env holds where and how to connect; \nConfig/Database.php just describes the shape \nCodeIgniter expects those settings to take.", "image": "slides/m2/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nModels and Query Builder", "image": "slides/m2/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "What Is a Model, Revisited", "text": "What Is a Model, Revisited\nIn Module 1 we defined the Model as the part that manages \ndata. Now we’ll see exactly what that looks like in code.\n•\nA CodeIgniter Model is a PHP class — one per table in \nthe database — that already knows how to talk to that \ntable, retrieving data, inserting new records, or \nupdating records.\n•\nIt extends CodeIgniter’s built-in Model class, which \nprovides ready-made methods for common queries \nand other database management functions.", "image": "slides/m2/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Creating Your First Model", "text": "Creating Your First Model\nCreate app/Models/TaskModel.php:\napp/Models/TaskModel.php\n<?php\nnamespace App\\Models;\nuse CodeIgniter\\Model;\nclass TaskModel extends Model\n{\nprotected $table = 'tasks';\n}", "image": "slides/m2/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "Anatomy of a Model", "text": "Anatomy of a Model\nA few protected properties tell CodeIgniter how to work with \nyour table (for complete list of properties, see CodeIgniter \ndocumentation):\nTaskModel.php\nprotected $table = 'tasks';\nprotected $primaryKey = 'id';\nprotected $allowedFields =\n['title', 'status'];\n$table — which table this Model manages\n$primaryKey — the column used to find a single row\n$allowedFields — which columns can be saved from user input", "image": "slides/m2/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Query Builder vs. Raw SQL", "text": "Query Builder vs. Raw SQL\nWriting raw SQL\n•\nYou build the query string \nby hand\n•\nEasy to forget escaping, \nopening the door to SQL \ninjection\n•\nSyntax differs slightly \nbetween database \nsystems\nUsing Query Builder\n•\nYou call PHP methods; \nCodeIgniter builds the \nSQL\n•\nValues are automatically \nescaped for you\n•\nThe same code works \nacross supported \ndatabases", "image": "slides/m2/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Retrieving All Records: findAll()", "text": "Retrieving All Records: findAll()\nEvery Model already has a findAll() method built in:\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$tasks = $model->findAll();\n$model is the instantiated TaskModel object using the new operator. The \ncode above can also be written as:\n$tasks = model(TaskModel::class)->findAll();\nWhich uses the CodeIgniter’s model() helper function.", "image": "slides/m2/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Retrieving One Record: find()", "text": "Retrieving One Record: find()\nPass a primary key value (usually a value from the id field) to \nget back a single row:\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$task = $model->find(3);\nReturns one record (or nothing, if no row has that id)", "image": "slides/m2/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "Filtering Results: where()", "text": "Filtering Results: where()\nAdd conditions before calling a retrieval method to filter which \nrecord/s will be returned:\n$pending = $model\n->where('status', 'pending')\n->findAll();\nOnly tasks whose status column equals 'pending' come back.", "image": "slides/m2/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Sorting Results: orderBy()", "text": "Sorting Results: orderBy()\nControl the order records come back in:\n$tasks = $model\n->orderBy('created_at', 'DESC')\n->findAll();\nDESC sorts data in descending order; use ASC to sort returned data in \nascending order.", "image": "slides/m2/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "Combining Methods: Chaining", "text": "Combining Methods: Chaining\nQuery Builder methods return the builder itself, so you can \nchain several functions together:\n$tasks = $model\n->where('status', 'pending')\n->orderBy('created_at', 'DESC')\n->findAll();", "image": "slides/m2/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Other Handy Query Builder Methods", "text": "Other Handy Query Builder Methods\nlike() — search for rows containing a keyword\nlimit() — cap the number of rows returned\ncountAllResults() — count matching rows instead of fetching \nthem\nfirst() — return just the first matching row", "image": "slides/m2/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Using the Model in a Controller", "text": "Using the Model in a Controller\nTwo valid styles: new TaskModel() or model() helper.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n<?php\nnamespace App\\Controllers;\nuse App\\Models\\TaskModel;\nclass Tasks extends BaseController\n{\npublic function index()\n{\n// Option 1: direct instantiation\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$tasks = $model->findAll();\n// Option 2: CodeIgniter model() helper\n// $tasks = model(TaskModel::class)->findAll();\n}\n}", "image": "slides/m2/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Passing Records to a View", "text": "Passing Records to a View\nSame idea as Module 1 — pass a $data array to view():\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\npublic function index()\n{\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$data['tasks'] = $model->findAll();\nreturn view('tasks/index', $data);\n}\n$data array is passed to the view \nwith a key named ‘tasks’. This key will \nthen serve as a variable inside the \nview.", "image": "slides/m2/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Displaying a List with foreach", "text": "Displaying a List with foreach\nCreate app/Views/tasks/index.php:\napp/Views/tasks/index.php\n<h1>My Tasks</h1>\n<ul>\n<?php foreach ($tasks as $task): ?>\n<li>\n<?= esc($task['title']) ?>\n— <?= esc($task['status']) ?>\n</li>\n<?php endforeach; ?>\n</ul>\nNote how foreach loop is used to \nread all records stored in the array. \nFurthermore, $tasks, which is the \narray key from the $data array \npassed to the view in the previous \nslide, is used here.", "image": "slides/m2/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Putting It Together: the Task List", "text": "Putting It Together: the Task List\nAdd the route, then visit the \npage:\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/tasks', 'Tasks::index');\nlocalhost:8080/tasks\nMy Tasks\n• Buy groceries — pending\n• Submit report — pending\n• Fix login bug — done", "image": "slides/m2/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Passing One Task to the View", "text": "Passing One Task to the View\nAdd a route with a numeric segment, then pass the value into \nshow($id):\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/tasks/(:num)', 'Tasks::show/$1');\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\npublic function show($id)\n{\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$data['task'] = $model->find($id);\nreturn view('tasks/show', $data);\n}\n(:num) accepts only a numeric \nURL segment. The $1 \nplaceholder passes that number \nto show($id). The $data key \nnamed task becomes the $task \nvariable inside tasks/show.php.", "image": "slides/m2/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Displaying One Task with show.php", "text": "Displaying One Task with show.php\nCreate app/Views/tasks/show.php \nso the single $task record can be \ndisplayed:\napp/Views/tasks/show.php\n<h1><?= esc($task['title']) ?></h1>\n<p>\nStatus: <?= esc($task['status']) ?>\n</p>\nlocalhost:8080/tasks/3\nFix login bug\nStatus: done", "image": "slides/m2/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Handling a Record That Doesn’t Exist", "text": "Handling a Record That Doesn’t Exist\nIf no row matches the id, find() simply returns null — it won’t \nthrow an error on its own.\nYour controller should check for that and respond sensibly:\nif ($data['task'] === null) {\nthrow new PageNotFoundException();\n}", "image": "slides/m2/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nA table name in the Model that \ndoesn’t match the real table\n•\nForgetting a column in \n$allowedFields, so saves \nsilently fail\n•\nNot checking for null before \nusing a single record\nRemember\n•\n.env holds real credentials; \nDatabase.php holds the \ndefaults\n•\nA Model wraps one table and \nknows how to query it\n•\nQuery Builder methods chain \ntogether and escape values for \nyou", "image": "slides/m2/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about database \nconfiguration, Models, or Query Builder?", "image": "slides/m2/036.webp"}], "3": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m3/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 3", "text": "MODULE 3\nForms, Validation, File Upload, and CRUD", "image": "slides/m3/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nBuild CodeIgniter forms that send data to controller \nmethods\n•\nValidate form input and show useful feedback\n•\nImplement create, read, update, and delete workflows\n•\nAccept and validate uploaded files safely\n•\nStore a public filename and display the uploaded file", "image": "slides/m3/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nForms and Validation\n•\nGET and POST routes\n•\nValidation rules\n•\nErrors and old input\nSubtopic 2\nCRUD Operations\n•\nCreate, update, delete\n•\nController and Model flow\n•\nSubtopic 3: File Upload\n•\nUpload validation\n•\nPublic file display", "image": "slides/m3/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nForms and Validation", "image": "slides/m3/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "Never Trust User Input", "text": "Never Trust User Input\nA form can send blank, incomplete, incorrect, or unsafe \nvalues.\nValidation is the checkpoint between submitted data and \nsaved data.\nThe controller validates first; the Model saves only after \nthe data passes the validation rules.\nFor the task manager sample that is used in the previous slides, the title \nmust exist before a new task is inserted.", "image": "slides/m3/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Create the Form Routes", "text": "Create the Form Routes\nAdd the routes before testing the \nform. One route shows the form; \nthe other receives the POST \nrequest. After writing the routes, \nuse php spark routes to confirm \nthey exist.\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/tasks/new', 'Tasks::new');\n$routes->post('/tasks', 'Tasks::create');\n$ php spark routes\nGET   /tasks/new   Tasks::new\nPOST  /tasks       Tasks::create", "image": "slides/m3/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Build the Add Task Form", "text": "Build the Add Task Form\nCreate a view \nthat posts to the \ncreate route.\nNotice the csrf_field() inside \nthe form. This is a \nCodeIgniter function that \ninserts a hidden field to the \nform that contains csrf token. \nThis secures your forms \nagainst Cross-Site Request \nForgery .\napp/Views/tasks/new.php\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"<?= site_url('tasks') ?>\">\n<?= csrf_field() ?>\n<input type=\"text\" name=\"title\">\n<button type=\"submit\">Add Task</button>\n</form>", "image": "slides/m3/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "The Controller Receives POST Data", "text": "The Controller Receives POST Data\nWhen the user clicks Add Task, the fields arrive in the \nrequest.\n•\nRead input through the request object, not directly \nfrom a global variable.\n•\nThe controller should still validate the value before \nsaving it.", "image": "slides/m3/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "Write Validation Rules", "text": "Write Validation Rules\nValidation rules describe what counts as valid for each field.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n$rules = [\n'title' => 'required|min_length[3]|max_length[150]',\n];", "image": "slides/m3/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "Common Validation Rules", "text": "Common Validation Rules\n•\nrequired - the field cannot be empty\n•\nmin_length[3] - at least 3 characters\n•\nmax_length[150] - no more than 150 characters\n•\nvalid_email - must look like an email address\n•\nnumeric - digits only\n•\nis_unique[tasks.title] - no duplicate title in the table\nNote: See CodeIgniter documentation for complete validation rules", "image": "slides/m3/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "If validation fails, return the form with the previous input", "text": "If validation fails, return the form with the previous input \nand error messages.\nRun Validation Before Saving\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\npublic function create()\n{\n$rules = ['title' => 'required|min_length[3]'];\nif (! $this->validate($rules)) {\nreturn redirect()->back()->withInput();\n}\n// Valid input can be saved here.\n}", "image": "slides/m3/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "Show the Error Beside the Field", "text": "Show the Error Beside the Field\n✓After a redirect with input, the view can print the field \nerror.\n✓The field name in validation and the field name in the \nform must match.", "image": "slides/m3/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "The old() helper keeps the submitted value visible after validation", "text": "The old() helper keeps the submitted value visible after validation \nfails. Show validation errors using validation_show_error().\nKeep the User's Old Input\napp/Views/tasks/new.php\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"<?= site_url('tasks') ?>\">\n<?= csrf_field() ?>\n<input type=\"text\" name=\"title\"\nvalue=\"<?= old('title') ?>\">\n<?= validation_show_error('title') ?>\n<button type=\"submit\">Add Task</button>\n</form>", "image": "slides/m3/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "A good form workflow validates first, keeps the", "text": "A good form workflow validates first, keeps the \nuser's input visible, and saves only after the \ndata is validated (safe).", "image": "slides/m3/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nCRUD Operations", "image": "slides/m3/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "CRUD Means Four Record Actions", "text": "CRUD Means Four Record Actions\n• Create - adds a new row.\n• Read - lists rows or shows one row.\n• Update - changes an existing row.\n• Delete - removes or deactivates a row.\n•\nIn CodeIgniter, the controller receives the request, and \nthe Model performs the table operation.", "image": "slides/m3/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "Create: Save a New Task", "text": "Create: Save a New Task\nAfter passing the \nvalidation, insert a row \nto the database table \nthrough the Model.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\nuse App\\Models\\TaskModel;\npublic function create()\n{\nif (! $this->validate(['title' => 'required'])) {\nreturn redirect()->back()->withInput();\n}\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$model->insert([\n'title'\n=> $this->request->getPost('title'),\n'status' => 'pending',\n]);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks');\n}", "image": "slides/m3/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Read, Update, and Delete Use the Same Model", "text": "Read, Update, and Delete Use the Same Model\nThe same TaskModel is used for all record actions. In each \ncontroller method, create the model object first, then call one \nclear model operation.\nTaskModel method map\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$tasks = $model->findAll();\n$task\n= $model->find($id);\n$model->update($id, $data);\n$model->delete($id);\n• findAll() reads \nmany records\n• find($id) reads one \nrecord\n• update() and \ndelete() change one \nrecord", "image": "slides/m3/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "Unsafe Shortcut vs. Clean CRUD Flow", "text": "Unsafe Shortcut vs. Clean CRUD Flow\nUnsafe shortcut\n•\nSave without validating\n•\nUse $_POST superglobal \nvariable directly\n•\nDelete with a normal link (it \nwill trigger GET method in \nroute)\n•\nRepeat SQL codes in views\nClean flow\n•\nValidate in controller\n•\nRead request values safely\n•\nUse POST for destructive \nwork (e.g.: updating or \ndeleting records)\n•\nCall Model methods only in \ncontroller/model code", "image": "slides/m3/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Update: Load the Existing Record", "text": "Update: Load the Existing Record\nLoad the record to be updated before showing the edit form.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$task = $model->find($id);\nreturn view('tasks/edit', ['task' => $task]);\nThe edit view receives data through the variable $task.", "image": "slides/m3/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Build the Edit Form", "text": "Build the Edit Form\nIn the edit form, use the old() helper function to display both \nthe validated data and the data from the database. \napp/Views/tasks/edit.php\n<input name=\"title\"\nvalue=\"<?= old('title', $task['title']) ?>\">\nold() keeps failed input (first parameter), then falls back to the saved value \n(second parameter).", "image": "slides/m3/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Update and Delete Controller Methods", "text": "Update and Delete Controller Methods\nUse the id from the route to change the selected record.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n$model = new TaskModel();\n// update method\n$model->update($id, [\n'title' => $this->request->getPost('title'),\n]);\n// delete method\n$model->delete($id);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks');\nUpdate changes one row; delete removes one row. Note that the id can be received \nthrough the 2nd segment in the URL.", "image": "slides/m3/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "SUBTOPIC 3", "text": "SUBTOPIC 3\nFile Upload", "image": "slides/m3/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "File Upload Forms Need Multipart Data", "text": "File Upload Forms Need Multipart Data\nA normal form will not send file bytes. Add enctype attribute \nto the form before using a file input element.\napp/Views/tasks/upload.php\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"<?= site_url('tasks/' . $task['id'] . '/upload') ?>\"\nenctype=\"multipart/form-data\">\n<?= csrf_field() ?>\n<input type=\"file\" name=\"attachment\">\n<button type=\"submit\">Upload</button>\n</form>\nWithout multipart/form-data, the file will not reach the request.", "image": "slides/m3/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "Validate Uploaded Files With File Rules", "text": "Validate Uploaded Files With File Rules\nFile fields use file-specific validation rules. The field name \nappears inside each rule.\nTasks.php\n$rules = [\n'attachment' => [\n'rules' => [\n'uploaded[attachment]',\n'max_size[attachment,2048]',\n'mime_in[attachment,image/jpeg,image/png]',\n],\n],\n];", "image": "slides/m3/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "File Upload Commands", "text": "File Upload Commands\n$file = $this->request->getFile('attachment')\n- Read the uploaded file object.\n$file->getRandomName()\n- Generate a safer server-side filename.\n$file->move(FCPATH . 'uploads', $newName)\n- Move to public/uploads for browser display.\nbase_url('uploads/' . $name)\n- Build the URL stored pages can display.", "image": "slides/m3/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Handle the Uploaded File Safely", "text": "Handle the Uploaded File Safely\nAfter validation, read the file and generate a safe name.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\nif (! $this->validateData([], $rules)) {\nreturn redirect()->back()->withInput();\n}\n$file = $this->request->getFile('attachment');\n$newName = $file->getRandomName();", "image": "slides/m3/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Move Public Files to public/uploads", "text": "Move Public Files to public/uploads\nTo store the uploaded file inside the public/uploads folder, \nuse the move() function.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n$uploadPath = FCPATH . 'uploads';\nif (! is_dir($uploadPath)) {\nmkdir($uploadPath, 0775, true);\n}\n$file->move($uploadPath, $newName);", "image": "slides/m3/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Prepare Images for Display", "text": "Prepare Images for Display\nAfter move(), resize or crop the uploaded image before \nshowing it as an avatar or product image.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n$image = service('image');\n$image->withFile(FCPATH . 'uploads/' . \n$newName)\n->fit(300, 300, 'center')\n->save(FCPATH . 'uploads/' . $newName);", "image": "slides/m3/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Save Only the Filename in the", "text": "Save Only the Filename in the\nDatabase\nSave the filename of the uploaded file to your database using \neither insert() or update() functions.\napp/Controllers/Tasks.php\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$model->update($id, [\n'attachment' => $newName,\n]);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks/' . $id);", "image": "slides/m3/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Display the Uploaded File", "text": "Display the Uploaded File\nBuild the public URL from the \nstored filename.\napp/Views/tasks/show.php\n<?php $url = base_url('uploads/' . \n$task['attachment']); ?>\n<img src=\"<?= esc($url, 'attr') ?>\">\nlocalhost:8080/tasks/3\nFix login bug\n[ attachment image preview ]", "image": "slides/m3/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Putting It Together: Upload Flow", "text": "Putting It Together: Upload Flow\nThe final upload method \nvalidates, moves the file, saves \nthe filename, and redirects.\nTasks.php\n$file = $this->request->getFile('attachment');\n$newName = $file->getRandomName();\n$file->move(FCPATH . 'uploads', $newName);\n$model = new TaskModel();\n$model->update($id, [\n'attachment' => $newName,\n]);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks/' . $id);\nlocalhost:8080/tasks/3\nFix login bug\nUpload complete\n[ attachment image preview ]", "image": "slides/m3/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Safe Upload Checklist", "text": "Safe Upload Checklist\nBefore accepting uploaded files, check type, size, name, \nstorage location, and display path.\nUpload checklist\n1. Form uses multipart/form-data.\n2. Field name matches validation rules.\n3. uploaded, max_size, and mime_in validate the file.\n4. File name is random, not user supplied.\n5. File is moved to the correct public folder.\n6. Image is prepared for display when needed.\n7. Only the filename or path is saved.\n8. The view builds the display URL safely.", "image": "slides/m3/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nSaving before validation\n•\nMismatched form field \nnames\n•\nDeleting records through \nGET links\n•\nUsing required instead of \nuploaded\n•\nSaving to writable but \ndisplaying with base_url\nRemember\n•\nValidate before save\n•\nold() keeps failed input \nvisible\n•\nModel methods perform \nCRUD\n•\nUse random names for files\n•\nStore filenames, not file \nbytes", "image": "slides/m3/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about validation, CRUD, or \nfile uploads?", "image": "slides/m3/036.webp"}], "4": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m4/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 4", "text": "MODULE 4\nCodeIgniter Authentication and Sessions", "image": "slides/m4/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nStore, retrieve, and remove small session values\n•\nUse flash data for one-time redirect messages\n•\nHash and verify passwords correctly\n•\nCreate a basic login and logout workflow\n•\nProtect routes with a CodeIgniter Filter", "image": "slides/m4/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nSessions\n•\nSession data\n•\nFlash messages\n•\nSession safety habits\nSubtopic 2\nAuthentication\n•\nPassword hashing\n•\nLogin and logout\n•\nProtected routes with \nfilters", "image": "slides/m4/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nSessions", "image": "slides/m4/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "A Session Gives HTTP a Memory", "text": "A Session Gives HTTP a Memory\nHTTP treats each page request as separate.\nA session ties small data to one visitor across requests.\nTypical examples include a logged-in user id, a username, or \na one-time message.\nDo not store passwords or large records in session data.", "image": "slides/m4/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Session Data Lives Across Requests", "text": "Session Data Lives Across Requests\nCodeIgniter starts the session \nservice for web requests.\nUse the session() helper to set \nor read values.\nThe browser keeps a session \ncookie; the app reads the \nmatching session data.\nThree forms of session\nRequests:\nRequest 1: set session value\nRequest 2: read the same value\nRequest 3: remove or destroy it", "image": "slides/m4/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Store and Read Session Values", "text": "Store and Read Session Values\nCodeIgniter starts session as \nsoon as any session-related \naction is made. No need to \nexplicitly call the \nsession_start().\nUse set() to remember a \nvalue and get() to read it \nlater.\nTasks.php\nsession()->set('username', 'admin');\n$name = session()->get('username');\n// or\n$name = session('username');\necho $name; // admin", "image": "slides/m4/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "Remove Session Values", "text": "Remove Session Values\nOther useful session functions are:\n•\nremove() clears one value.\n•\ndestroy() clears the whole session.\nUse destroy() during logout because all login state should end at once.", "image": "slides/m4/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "Flash Data Is for One-Time Messages", "text": "Flash Data Is for One-Time Messages\nData stored in as a flash data survives one more request, so \nit is ideal after a redirect. After a flash data is used, it is \nautomaticaly removed.\nTasks.php\nsession()->setFlashdata(\n'message',\n'Task added successfully!'\n);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks');", "image": "slides/m4/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "To retrieve a flash data, e.g. the message on the", "text": "To retrieve a flash data, e.g. the message on the \ndestination page after the redirect, use getFlashdata().\nDisplay the Flash Message in the View\napp/Views/tasks/index.php\n<?php if (session()->getFlashdata('message')): ?>\n<p class=\"success\">\n<?= session()->getFlashdata('message') ?>\n</p>\n<?php endif ?>", "image": "slides/m4/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "Practical Example: Confirmation Message", "text": "Practical Example: Confirmation Message\nThe workflow in using flash data is as follows:\n1. The task is saved first.\n2. The controller sets flash data.\n3. The redirect returns to the list page.\n4. The list page shows the message once.", "image": "slides/m4/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "Session Safety Habits", "text": "Session Safety Habits\n•\nStore only small values \nneeded for the next requests.\n•\nStore a user id, not the user's \npassword.\n•\nClear login session data on \nlogout.\n•\nUse flash data for messages \nthat should disappear.\nGood: session user_id\nGood: session username\nBad: session password\nBad: entire user table", "image": "slides/m4/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Common Session Errors", "text": "Common Session Errors\n•\nThe flash message is set after the redirect.\n•\nThe view checks the wrong flash-data key.\n•\nLogout removes one key but leaves other login \nvalues.\n•\nLarge arrays are stored in the session instead of the \ndatabase.", "image": "slides/m4/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "Session data lasts the visit; flash data lasts", "text": "Session data lasts the visit; flash data lasts \nexactly one request past the one that set it.", "image": "slides/m4/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nAuthentication", "image": "slides/m4/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "Authentication Checks Who the User Is", "text": "Authentication Checks Who the User Is\nA login form collects a username and password.\n•\nThe app finds the user record by username.\n•\nThe typed password is verified against a stored hash.\n•\nIf the check passes, the app stores login state in the \nsession.", "image": "slides/m4/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "Prepare a Minimal users Table", "text": "Prepare a Minimal users Table\nA login table needs an id, username, and hashed password.\nA sample user table is as follows:\nusers table\nid          INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY\nusername    VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE\npassword    VARCHAR(255)\n-- password stores a hash, not plain text", "image": "slides/m4/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Hash Passwords Before Saving", "text": "Hash Passwords Before Saving\nNever save a plain-text password. Hash it first, then store the \nhash.\nUser setup example\n$hash = password_hash(\n$plainPassword,\nPASSWORD_DEFAULT\n);\n// Save $hash in the password column.\npassword_hash() creates the stored hash.\nPASSWORD_DEFAULT lets PHP choose a strong default.\nThe plain password is never saved.", "image": "slides/m4/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "Create UserModel and One Test User", "text": "Create UserModel and One Test User\nBefore the login form works, the users table needs a Model \nand one hashed demo account.\napp/Models/UserModel.php and setup example\nuse CodeIgniter\\Model;\nclass UserModel extends Model\n{\nprotected $table = 'users';\nprotected $allowedFields = ['username', 'password'];\n}\n$model = new UserModel();\n$model->insert([\n'username' => 'admin',\n'password' => password_hash('secret123', PASSWORD_DEFAULT),\n]);\nUsername:\nadmin\nTyped password:\nsecret123\nStored value:\nhash only", "image": "slides/m4/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Manual Page Checks vs. A Filter", "text": "Manual Page Checks vs. A Filter\nManual checks\n• Repeated in every \nmethod\n• Easy to forget\n• Harder to update later\n• Scattered login logic\nFilter approach\n•\nOne class owns the check\n•\nRoutes declare protection\n•\nRedirects happen before \ncontroller logic\n•\nEasier to reuse", "image": "slides/m4/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Build the Login Form", "text": "Build the Login Form\nThe form posts the typed credentials to the login route.\napp/Views/auth/login.php\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"<?= site_url('login') ?>\">\n<?= csrf_field() ?>\n<input name=\"username\">\n<input type=\"password\" name=\"password\">\n<button type=\"submit\">Log In</button>\n</form>\nThe controller will read username and password from the request.", "image": "slides/m4/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Find the User Record", "text": "Find the User Record\nUse a UserModel for the users table, then search for the \nposted username.\nAuth.php\nuse App\\Models\\UserModel;\n$model = new UserModel();\n$username = $this->request->getPost('username');\n$user = $model->where('username', $username)->first();\nThis follows the same pattern as TaskModel: import the class, create the \nobject, then call a query method.", "image": "slides/m4/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "Verify the Password Hash", "text": "Verify the Password Hash\npassword_verify() compares the typed password to the \nstored hash.\nAuth.php\nif (! $user || ! password_verify($password, $user['password'])) \n{\nsession()->setFlashdata('error', 'Invalid login.');\nreturn redirect()->back()->withInput();\n}\nDo not compare the typed password with == or === especially if using hashed password.", "image": "slides/m4/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Remember the Logged-In User", "text": "Remember the Logged-In User\nAfter login, keep only small login values in session.\nAuth.php\nsession()->set([\n'isLoggedIn' => true,\n'user_id'\n=> $user['id'],\n'username'\n=> $user['username'],\n]);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks');\nThe password is not stored in the session.", "image": "slides/m4/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "Add a Logout Route and", "text": "Add a Logout Route and\nMethod\nLogout destroys the session, then redirects to login.\nAuth.php\npublic function logout()\n{\nsession()->destroy();\nreturn redirect()->to('/login');\n}\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/logout', 'Auth::logout');\nThis uses the same destroy() call introduced earlier in this module.", "image": "slides/m4/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Protecting Pages With a Filter", "text": "Protecting Pages With a Filter\nA Filter runs before the controller. If the user is not logged in, \nredirect to login before the protected page loads.\nFilter behavior flow\nRequest -> Filter -> Controller\nIf isLoggedIn is true:\ncontinue to controller\nIf isLoggedIn is missing:\nredirect to /login", "image": "slides/m4/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Writing a Filter", "text": "Writing a Filter\n•\nThe before() method checks session state before the \ncontroller runs.\n•\nIf isLoggedIn value in session is missing, the filter returns \na redirect response, redirecting to login.\n•\nThe after() method will execute after the Controller runs \nright before the response of the Controller method is sent \nto the browser for viewing.", "image": "slides/m4/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "AuthFilter: the Full Class", "text": "AuthFilter: the Full Class\nCreate the filter class in app/Filters/AuthFilter.php.\napp/Filters/AuthFilter.php\n<?php\nnamespace App\\Filters;\nuse CodeIgniter\\Filters\\FilterInterface;\nuse CodeIgniter\\HTTP\\RequestInterface;\nuse CodeIgniter\\HTTP\\ResponseInterface;\nclass AuthFilter implements FilterInterface\n{\npublic function before(RequestInterface $request, $arguments = null)\n{\nif (! session()->get('isLoggedIn')) {\nreturn redirect()->to('/login');\n}\n}\npublic function after(RequestInterface $request, ResponseInterface $response, $arguments = null) {}\n}", "image": "slides/m4/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Register the Filter Alias", "text": "Register the Filter Alias\nGive the filter a short alias before using it in a route.\napp/Config/Filters.php\npublic array $aliases = [\n'auth' => \\App\\Filters\\AuthFilter::class,\n];", "image": "slides/m4/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Apply the Filter to Routes", "text": "Apply the Filter to Routes\nAttach the auth alias to every route that requires login. The \nfilter protects the page before the controller runs. This is \nauthentication-based access control; role-based permissions \nneed additional role checks.\napp/Config/Routes.php\n$routes->get('/tasks', 'Tasks::index', [\n'filter' => 'auth',\n]);", "image": "slides/m4/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Putting It Together: Login Flow", "text": "Putting It Together: Login Flow\nSuccessful login stores session data, \nthen redirects to the protected page.\nAuth.php\nif ($user && \npassword_verify($password, $user['password'])) {\nsession()->set([\n'isLoggedIn' => true,\n'user_id'\n=> $user['id'],\n'username'\n=> $user['username'],\n]);\nreturn redirect()->to('/tasks');\n}\nlocalhost:8080/tasks\nMy Tasks\nWelcome, admin\nLogout", "image": "slides/m4/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Test Access Control", "text": "Test Access Control\nTry the protected route before \nand after login.\nManual test\n1. Log out.\n2. Visit /tasks.\n3. Confirm redirect to /login.\n4. Log in.\n5. Visit /tasks again.\nlocalhost:8080/tasks\nRedirected to /login\nYou must log in to view this page.", "image": "slides/m4/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Safe Authentication Checklist", "text": "Safe Authentication Checklist\nEven the simplest web application must observe proper \nauthentication and access-control habits. Here are the things \nto remember for authentication:\nChecklist\n1. Validate login fields.\n2. Look up the username.\n3. Verify with password_verify().\n4. Store only small session values.\n5. Protect routes with a filter.\n6. Destroy the session on logout.", "image": "slides/m4/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nSaving plain-text passwords\n•\nUsing == instead of \npassword_verify()\n•\nForgetting the filter alias\n•\nProtecting the page but not \nthe route\n•\nLeaving login data after \nlogout\nRemember\n•\nSessions give HTTP \nmemory\n•\nFlash data is one-time\n•\nHash and verify are a pair\n•\nA Filter centralizes access \nchecks\n•\nLogout destroys login state", "image": "slides/m4/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about sessions, login, or \nprotected routes?", "image": "slides/m4/036.webp"}], "5": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m5/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 5", "text": "MODULE 5\nLaravel Foundations", "image": "slides/m5/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nSet up a Laravel project and run it locally\n•\nIdentify how Laravel structure compares with \nCodeIgniter\n•\nUse Artisan commands to inspect and generate \napplication files\n•\nCreate Laravel routes, controllers, Blade views, and \npass data to views", "image": "slides/m5/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nLaravel Setup\n•\nInstalling and creating a \nproject\n•\nProject structure and .env\n•\nArtisan and the \ndevelopment server\nSubtopic 2\nLaravel MVC Basics\n•\nRoutes and request flow\n•\nControllers and Blade \nviews\n•\nPassing data from \ncontroller to view", "image": "slides/m5/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nLaravel Setup", "image": "slides/m5/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "Why Learn Laravel After CodeIgniter?", "text": "Why Learn Laravel After CodeIgniter?\n•\nYou already know the main idea: a framework gives \nstructure to server-side PHP applications.\n•\nLaravel uses the same MVC thinking, but it is more \nconvention-driven and tool-assisted.\n•\nThe goal is not to memorize a second framework from \nzero; it is to transfer what you already know into Laravel's \nworkflow.", "image": "slides/m5/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Creating a New Laravel Project", "text": "Creating a New Laravel Project\nCreate the project from the \nfolder where you keep your web \napplications.\nThe Laravel installer creates a \nready-to-run project with routes, \nviews, configuration, and Artisan \nalready included.\n$ composer global require \nlaravel/installer\n$ laravel new myproject\n$ cd myproject\nApplication ready.", "image": "slides/m5/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Project Structure at a Glance", "text": "Project Structure at a Glance\nA new Laravel project has more \nfolders than the first CodeIgniter \nexamples.\nFor this module, students only \nneed to recognize the folders that \naffect routing, controllers, views, \nconfiguration, and public access.\nmyproject/\napp/\nHttp/Controllers/\nbootstrap/\nconfig/\ndatabase/\npublic/\nresources/views/\nroutes/web.php\nstorage/\nvendor/", "image": "slides/m5/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "Why Laravel Uses Conventions", "text": "Why Laravel Uses Conventions\nLaravel expects common files to live in predictable \nplaces.\n•\nRoutes normally live in routes/web.php\n•\nControllers live in app/Http/Controllers\n•\nBlade views live in resources/views\n•\nThe public folder is the web entry point\n•\nBecause the locations are predictable, Laravel tools and \ndevelopers can find files quickly.", "image": "slides/m5/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "Configuring the .env File", "text": "Configuring the .env File\nOpen .env in the project root and check these values:\n.env\nAPP_NAME=\"Student Portal\"\nAPP_ENV=local\nAPP_DEBUG=true\nAPP_URL=http://localhost:8000\nDB_CONNECTION=sqlite", "image": "slides/m5/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "The application URL tells Laravel what address the", "text": "The application URL tells Laravel what address the \nproject expects during development.\nSetting the Application URL\n.env\nAPP_URL=http://localhost:8000\n# If you use a local virtual host:\nAPP_URL=http://myproject.test", "image": "slides/m5/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "How Laravel Uses These Settings", "text": "How Laravel Uses These Settings\n•\n.env holds values that can change per computer or server.\n•\nConfiguration files in config/ read those values and give \nLaravel a consistent place to look.\nThis is the same pattern in CodeIgniter: local settings stay separate from application code.", "image": "slides/m5/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "Running Laravel Locally", "text": "Running Laravel Locally\n•\nLaravel includes Artisan, a \ncommand-line tool for \nframework tasks.\n•\nFor first pages, php artisan \nserve is the simplest local \nserver command.\n•\nIf the project uses frontend \nassets, Laravel's dev script can \nrun more tools together.\n$ php artisan serve\nServer running on \nhttp://127.0.0.1:8000\n$ composer run dev\nStarts Laravel's full \ndevelopment workflow", "image": "slides/m5/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Common Setup Errors", "text": "Common Setup Errors\n•\nlaravel is not recognized - Composer's global bin \nfolder is not on PATH\n•\ncomposer is not recognized - Composer is not \ninstalled or terminal was not restarted\n•\nPort already in use - another server is already using \nport 8000\n•\nWhite error page - read the Laravel error message, \nthen check .env and the file you edited", "image": "slides/m5/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "Laravel feels easier once you stop looking for", "text": "Laravel feels easier once you stop looking for \nCodeIgniter file names and start looking for the \nsame MVC responsibilities.", "image": "slides/m5/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nLaravel MVC Basics", "image": "slides/m5/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "Laravel Still Follows MVC Thinking", "text": "Laravel Still Follows MVC Thinking\nA request arrives through a route.\n•\nThe route may respond directly or send the request to \na controller.\n•\nThe controller prepares the data and returns a Blade \nview.\n•\nThe Blade view displays HTML back to the browser.", "image": "slides/m5/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "Writing Your First Route", "text": "Writing Your First Route\nStep 1: open routes/web.php.\nStep 2: add one simple route.\nStep 3: visit /about and confirm the response.\nroutes/web.php\n<?php\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route;\nRoute::get('/about', function () {\nreturn 'This is the about page.';\n});", "image": "slides/m5/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Laravel Route Anatomy", "text": "Laravel Route Anatomy\nA route connects a URL pattern to code that returns a \nresponse.\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::get('/about', function () {\nreturn 'This is the about page.';\n});\nRoute::get - responds to a normal browser page request\n'/about' - the URL path after the domain name\nfunction () - the code Laravel runs when that URL is visited", "image": "slides/m5/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "CI vs. Laravel MVC Map", "text": "CI vs. Laravel MVC Map\nCodeIgniter pattern\n•\napp/Config/Routes.php\n•\napp/Controllers/Pages.php\n•\napp/Views/about.php\n•\nview('about')\nLaravel pattern\n• routes/web.php\n• app/Http/Controllers/ \nPageController.php\n• resources/views/ \nabout.blade.php\n• view('about')", "image": "slides/m5/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Returning a View Directly", "text": "Returning a View Directly\nUse Route::view for simple static pages:\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::view('/about', 'about');\nThis is useful when the page does not need controller logic yet.", "image": "slides/m5/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Passing Data from a Route", "text": "Passing Data from a Route\nRoute::view can also pass small data arrays to a Blade view:\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::view('/about', 'about', [\n'course' => 'IT0049',\n]);\nThe key 'course' becomes a $course variable in the Blade file.", "image": "slides/m5/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Using Route Parameters", "text": "Using Route Parameters\nA route can capture part of the URL and pass it into the \nfunction:\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::get('/tasks/{id}', function ($id) {\nreturn \"Task ID: \" . $id;\n});\nVisiting /tasks/15 makes $id contain 15.", "image": "slides/m5/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "Creating a Controller with Artisan", "text": "Creating a Controller with Artisan\nArtisan can generate a controller file in the correct folder:\nTerminal\nphp artisan make:controller PageController\n# Created:\napp/Http/Controllers/PageController.php\nUse Artisan when Laravel already has a convention for the file you \nneed.", "image": "slides/m5/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Writing a Controller Method", "text": "Writing a Controller Method\nA controller method prepares the response for one page:\napp/Http/Controllers/PageController.php\n<?php\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\nclass PageController extends Controller\n{\npublic function about()\n{\nreturn view('about');\n}\n}", "image": "slides/m5/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "Artisan Commands", "text": "Artisan Commands\nphp artisan list\n- Show available commands\nphp artisan route:list\n- Show registered routes\nphp artisan make:controller PageController\n- Create a controller\nphp artisan serve\n- Run a local development server", "image": "slides/m5/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Connecting the Route to the Controller", "text": "Connecting the Route to the Controller\nAfter the controller exists, point /about to \nPageController::about.\nThis will make the route to reach the controller method.\nroutes/web.php\n<?php\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route;\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\PageController;\nRoute::get('/about', [PageController::class, 'about']);", "image": "slides/m5/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Passing Data to Views", "text": "Passing Data to Views\nPass an array as the second argument of view():\nPageController.php\npublic function about()\n{\n$data = [\n'course' => 'Web System Technologies',\n'module' => 'Laravel Foundations',\n];\nreturn view('about', $data);\n}", "image": "slides/m5/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Creating Your First Blade View", "text": "Creating Your First Blade View\nCreate resources/views/about.blade.php:\nresources/views/about.blade.php\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<title>About</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h1>{{ $course }}</h1>\n<p>{{ $module }}</p>\n</body>\n</html>", "image": "slides/m5/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Putting It Together: the About Page", "text": "Putting It Together: the About Page\nThe route, controller method, and \nBlade view produce the About \npage.\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::get('/about',\n[PageController::class, 'about']);\nlocalhost:8000/about\nWeb System Technologies\nLaravel Foundations", "image": "slides/m5/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Showing One Task's Details", "text": "Showing One Task's Details\nThe route passes {id} into the \ncontroller method.\nroutes/web.php and PageController.php\nRoute::get('/tasks/{id}',\n[PageController::class, 'task']);\npublic function task($id)\n{\n$data = [\n'id' => $id,\n'title' => 'Buy groceries',\n];\nreturn view('task', $data);\n}\nlocalhost:8000/tasks/15\nBuy groceries\nTask ID: 15", "image": "slides/m5/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Safe Blade Output", "text": "Safe Blade Output\nUse double curly braces for normal page output.\nBlade escapes these values before printing them.\nresources/views/task.blade.php\n<h1>{{ $title }}</h1>\n<p>Task ID: {{ $id }}</p>\n{{-- Avoid raw output for user input --}}\n{!! $title !!}", "image": "slides/m5/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nPutting routes in the wrong \nfile\n•\nMisspelling the controller \nclass\n•\nForgetting to import the \ncontroller\n•\nUsing about.php instead of \nabout.blade.php\nRemember\n•\nroutes/web.php maps the \nURL\n•\nControllers coordinate page \nlogic\n•\nBlade views live in \nresources/views\n•\n{{ }} safely prints view data", "image": "slides/m5/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about Laravel setup, Artisan, \nroutes, controllers, or Blade?", "image": "slides/m5/034.webp"}], "6": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m6/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 6", "text": "MODULE 6\nLaravel Data Layer", "image": "slides/m6/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nConfigure Laravel database settings in .env\n•\nCreate tables using migrations\n•\nPopulate sample records using seeders\n•\nRetrieve records with Eloquent models\n•\nDisplay database records through controllers and \nBlade views", "image": "slides/m6/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nDatabase Setup in Laravel\n•\n.env configuration\n•\nMigrations and seeders\n•\nRollback, refresh, and sample \ndata\nSubtopic 2\nEloquent ORM\n•\nModels and Eloquent queries\n•\nController-model-view flow\n•\nDisplaying records in Blade", "image": "slides/m6/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nDatabase Setup in Laravel", "image": "slides/m6/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "The Data Layer Turns Pages Into Applications", "text": "The Data Layer Turns Pages Into Applications\n•\nLaravel keeps database settings in .env, table \nstructure in migrations, sample data in seeders, and \nrecord logic in Eloquent models.\n•\nEloquent is the model counterpart of CodeIgniter \nModels", "image": "slides/m6/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Configure the Database in .env", "text": "Configure the Database in .env\n•\nFor a simple local classroom \nsetup, SQLite keeps \neverything inside one file.\n•\nFor MySQL, the same Laravel \ncode can work after changing \nthe connection values.\n$ type nul > \ndatabase\\database.sqlite\n$ php artisan migrate\nINFO  Preparing database.\nINFO  Running migrations.", "image": "slides/m6/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Database Files You Touch", "text": "Database Files You Touch\n•\nLaravel separates schema, \nsample data, and model logic \ninto predictable files.\n•\nKnowing these folders \nprevents students from placing \ndatabase code inside views.\nstudent-portal/\n.env\ndatabase/\nmigrations/\nseeders/\napp/\nModels/\napp/Http/Controllers/\nresources/views/\nroutes/web.php", "image": "slides/m6/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "Migrations Version Table Changes", "text": "Migrations Version Table Changes\nA migration describes how a table should be created or \nchanged.\n•\nLaravel records which migrations already ran, so each \ndeveloper or server can rebuild the same schema.\n•\nFor the task manager, the first custom migration creates \nthe tasks table.", "image": "slides/m6/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "Create the tasks Table Migration", "text": "Create the tasks Table Migration\nStep 1: generate the migration file.\nStep 2: define each column the task app needs.\nStep 3: run migrate and confirm the table exists.\ndatabase/migrations/...create_tasks_table.php\nSchema::create('tasks', function (Blueprint $table) {\n$table->id();\n$table->string('title', 150);\n$table->string('status', 20)->default('pending');\n$table->timestamps();\n});", "image": "slides/m6/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "Choose columns based on what the application needs to", "text": "Choose columns based on what the application needs to \ndisplay, search, or update.\nCommon Migration Column Choices\nMigration column examples\n$table->id();\n$table->string('title', 100);\n$table->text('notes')->nullable();\n$table->boolean('is_active')->default(false);\n$table->timestamps();", "image": "slides/m6/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "Run, Roll Back, and Refresh Migrations", "text": "Run, Roll Back, and Refresh Migrations\nOther migrate functions are:\n•\nphp artisan migrate - runs pending migrations.\n•\nmigrate:rollback undoes the last batch.\n•\nmigrate:fresh rebuilds all tables from zero.\n!!! Use destructive reset commands only in local development, never on real production \ndata.", "image": "slides/m6/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "Seeders Create Predictable Sample Records", "text": "Seeders Create Predictable Sample Records\n•\nA seeder inserts sample \nrecords so the application has \ndata to display.\n•\nGood seed data makes \ndemonstrations repeatable \nand easier to debug.\n$ php artisan make:seeder \nTaskSeeder\n$ php artisan db:seed --\nclass=TaskSeeder\nINFO  Database seeding \ncompleted successfully.", "image": "slides/m6/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Write the Task Seeder", "text": "Write the Task Seeder\ndatabase/seeders/TaskSeeder.php\nuse App\\Models\\Task;\npublic function run(): void\n{\nTask::create([\n'title' => 'Buy groceries',\n'status' => 'pending',\n]);\nTask::create([\n'title' => 'Submit report',\n'status' => 'pending',\n]);\nTask::create([\n'title' => 'Fix login bug',", "image": "slides/m6/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "Laravel database work is easiest when you can", "text": "Laravel database work is easiest when you can \nsay which file owns each job: settings, schema, \nsample data, model, controller, or view.", "image": "slides/m6/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nEloquent ORM", "image": "slides/m6/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "Eloquent Turns Rows Into PHP Objects", "text": "Eloquent Turns Rows Into PHP Objects\nA model represents one database table.\n• A model object represents one record from that table.\n• Eloquent lets controllers retrieve and update records \nwithout writing raw SQL for every page.", "image": "slides/m6/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "Create the Task Model", "text": "Create the Task Model\nGenerate the model in \napp/Models by typing the \ncommand:\nphp artisan make:model Task\n$ php artisan make:model Task\nCreated: app/Models/Task.php", "image": "slides/m6/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Allow Safe Mass Assignment", "text": "Allow Safe Mass Assignment\nTell Laravel which columns create() and update() may fill:\napp/Models/Task.php\nclass Task extends Model\n{\nprotected $fillable = [\n'title',\n'status',\n];\n}\n$fillable protects create/update fields (same with $allowedFields in CodeIgniter Models)", "image": "slides/m6/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "CI vs. Laravel Data Layer Map", "text": "CI vs. Laravel Data Layer Map\nCodeIgniter pattern\n• .env database settings\n• Migration or SQL schema\n• app/Models/TaskModel.php\n• Query Builder or model methods\nLaravel pattern\n• .env database settings\n• database/migrations\n• app/Models/Task.php\n• Eloquent model queries", "image": "slides/m6/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Retrieve All Task Records", "text": "Retrieve All Task Records\nRead all rows, then pass them to Blade.\nTaskController.php\n$tasks = Task::all();\nreturn view('tasks.index', compact('tasks'));\nTask::all() returns many Task rows (Same as findAll() in \nCodeIgniter). compact() is a PHP function to create array from \nexisting variables.", "image": "slides/m6/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Pass Records to a Blade View", "text": "Pass Records to a Blade View\nThe array key becomes the variable name inside the Blade \nfile:\nTaskController.php\nreturn view('tasks.index', [\n'tasks' => $tasks,\n]);\nThe view receives $tasks and can loop through each record.", "image": "slides/m6/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Loop Through Records in Blade", "text": "Loop Through Records in Blade\nA foreach loop prints each model object in the collection:\nresources/views/tasks/index.blade.php\n<h1>My Tasks</h1>\n<ul>\n@foreach ($tasks as $task)\n<li>{{ $task->title }} — {{ $task->status }}</li>\n@endforeach\n</ul>\nUse {{ }} so Blade escapes output safely.", "image": "slides/m6/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "Filter Pending Tasks", "text": "Filter Pending Tasks\nEloquent queries can add conditions before retrieving \nrecords:\nTaskController.php\n$tasks = Task::where('status', 'pending')\n->orderBy('created_at', 'desc')\n->get();\nreturn view('tasks.index', [\n'tasks' => $tasks,\n]);\nwhere() filters rows; orderBy() controls display order; get() runs the query.", "image": "slides/m6/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Find One Record by ID", "text": "Find One Record by ID\nUse findOrFail() when a missing record should become a 404 \npage:\nTaskController.php\npublic function show($id)\n{\n$task = Task::findOrFail($id);\nreturn view('tasks.show', [\n'task' => $task,\n]);\n}", "image": "slides/m6/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "Eloquent Commands", "text": "Eloquent Commands\nTask::all()\n- Retrieve all records\nTask::where(...)->get()\n- Retrieve matching records\nTask::findOrFail($id)\n- Retrieve one record or show 404\nTask::create([...])\n- Insert a new record using fillable fields", "image": "slides/m6/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Connect the List Route", "text": "Connect the List Route\nThe route connects the URL to the controller method:\nroutes/web.php\n<?php\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route;\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\TaskController;\nRoute::get('/tasks', [TaskController::class, 'index']);\nRoute::get('/tasks/{id}', [TaskController::class, 'show']);", "image": "slides/m6/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Show Completion Status", "text": "Show Completion Status\nA Blade condition can display different text based on the \ndatabase value:\ntasks/index.blade.php\n@foreach ($tasks as $task)\n<li>\n{{ $task->title }}\n@if ($task->status === 'done')\n<span>Done</span>\n@else\n<span>Pending</span>\n@endif\n</li>\n@endforeach", "image": "slides/m6/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Create the Details View", "text": "Create the Details View\nThe details view receives one Task object:\nresources/views/tasks/show.blade.php\n<h1>{{ $task->title }}</h1>\n<p>Status: {{ $task->status }}</p>", "image": "slides/m6/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Putting It Together: Task List", "text": "Putting It Together: Task List\nVisit the list route after migrating \nand seeding:\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::get('/tasks', [\nTaskController::class, 'index'\n]);\nlocalhost:8000/tasks\nMy Tasks\nBuy groceries — Pending\nSubmit report — Pending\nFix login bug — Done", "image": "slides/m6/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Putting It Together: Task Details", "text": "Putting It Together: Task Details\nThe details route retrieves one \nrecord and sends it to one Blade \nview.\nTaskController.php\npublic function show($id)\n{\n$task = Task::findOrFail($id);\nreturn view('tasks.show', \ncompact('task'));\n}\nlocalhost:8000/tasks/3\nFix login bug\nStatus: done", "image": "slides/m6/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Use Tinker for Data Checks", "text": "Use Tinker for Data Checks\n•\nTinker lets you run small \nLaravel commands from the \nterminal.\n•\nUse it to confirm that the \ndatabase has records before \ndebugging the view.\n$ php artisan tinker\n>>> App\\Models\\Task::count();\n3\n>>> App\\Models\\Task::first()->title;\n\"Buy groceries\"", "image": "slides/m6/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n• Wrong DB_DATABASE path\n• Migration not yet run\n• Model namespace missing\n• $fillable not set for create()\n• Query written inside Blade\nRemember\n• .env configures the connection\n• Migrations create tables\n• Seeders create sample rows\n• Eloquent retrieves model objects\n• Controllers pass records to Blade", "image": "slides/m6/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about migrations, seeders, \nEloquent models, or displaying \ndatabase records?", "image": "slides/m6/034.webp"}], "7": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m7/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 7", "text": "MODULE 7\nLaravel Forms, Validation, and File Upload", "image": "slides/m7/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nBuild Laravel form submission routes\n•\nUse request validation and display validation feedback\n•\nRedisplay old input after validation errors\n•\nUpdate records through controller methods\n•\nValidate, store, display, and replace uploaded files \nsafely", "image": "slides/m7/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nForms and Request \nValidation\n•\nGET and POST routes\n•\nCSRF protection\n•\nErrors and old input\nSubtopic 2\nCRUD and File Upload\n•\nEdit and update workflows\n•\nStorage and file validation\n•\nDisplaying uploaded files \nsafely", "image": "slides/m7/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nForms and Request Validation", "image": "slides/m7/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "Two Routes Per Form", "text": "Two Routes Per Form\nA form workflow usually needs one route to show the form \nand another route to receive the submitted data.\nGET displays the form page.\nPOST sends the user's input to the controller.\nThe controller validates the input before storing or \nupdating records.", "image": "slides/m7/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Create and Store Routes", "text": "Create and Store Routes\nFirst generate the controller. The \nnext slide registers the GET and \nPOST routes before you verify \nthem.\n$ php artisan make:controller \nTaskController\nINFO  Controller created \nsuccessfully.", "image": "slides/m7/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Write the Form Routes", "text": "Write the Form Routes\nWrite the routes in \nroutes/web.php:\nroutes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\TaskController;\nRoute::get('/tasks/create',\n[TaskController::class, 'create']\n)->name('tasks.create');\nRoute::post('/tasks',\n[TaskController::class, 'store']\n)->name('tasks.store');", "image": "slides/m7/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "CSRF Token Required", "text": "CSRF Token Required\nLaravel protects web forms from cross-site request forgery.\n•\nEvery POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE form in web \nroutes should include @csrf.\n•\nWithout it, Laravel rejects the form submission.", "image": "slides/m7/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "Build a Create Form", "text": "Build a Create Form\nThe form posts to the named store route:\nresources/views/tasks/create.blade.php\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('tasks.store') }}\">\n@csrf\n<label>Title</label>\n<input name=\"title\" value=\"{{ old('title') }}\">\n<button type=\"submit\">Save</button>\n</form>", "image": "slides/m7/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "Laravel can inject the current HTTP request into a", "text": "Laravel can inject the current HTTP request into a \ncontroller method.\nThe Controller Receives the Request\nTaskController.php\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n// Validate the submitted form data here.\n}", "image": "slides/m7/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "Validate Before Saving", "text": "Validate Before Saving\n•\nThe validate() method checks the incoming request.\n•\nIf validation fails, Laravel automatically redirects back \nwith errors and old input.", "image": "slides/m7/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "Rules should match the table columns and the application", "text": "Rules should match the table columns and the application \nrequirement:\nWrite the Validation Rules\nTaskController.php\n$validated = $request->validate([\n'title' => ['required', 'string', 'max:100'],\n]);\nIf validation fails, Laravel redirects back with errors.", "image": "slides/m7/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Display Validation Feedback", "text": "Display Validation Feedback\n•\nBlade can show errors beside the field that failed \nvalidation.\n•\n@error('title') checks whether the title field has an error.\n•\n{{ $message }} prints Laravel's validation message.\n•\nold('title') restores what the user typed after a failed \nsubmission.\n•\nThis makes the form easier to correct without starting \nover.", "image": "slides/m7/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "A good form workflow protects the database,", "text": "A good form workflow protects the database, \nguides the user, and keeps the input visible \nwhen something needs correction.", "image": "slides/m7/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nCRUD and File Upload", "image": "slides/m7/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "Edit and Update Follow the Same Pattern", "text": "Edit and Update Follow the Same Pattern\nThe edit page loads an existing record.\n•\nThe form shows the current values.\n•\nThe update route receives the edited values.\n•\nThe controller validates the values before saving changes.", "image": "slides/m7/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "Define Edit, Update, and Upload Routes", "text": "Define Edit, Update, and Upload Routes\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::get('/tasks/{task}/edit',\n[TaskController::class, 'edit']\n)->name('tasks.edit');\nRoute::patch('/tasks/{task}',\n[TaskController::class, 'update']\n)->name('tasks.update');\nRoute::post('/tasks/{task}/upload',\n[TaskController::class, 'upload']\n)->name('tasks.upload');", "image": "slides/m7/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Load the Existing Record", "text": "Load the Existing Record\nThe edit method sends the selected record to the form view:\nTaskController.php\nuse App\\Models\\Task;\npublic function edit(Task $task)\n{\nreturn view('tasks.edit', [\n'task' => $task,\n]);\n}\nThe URL value selects the record.\nThe controller sends the record to Blade.\nThe form shows the current values.", "image": "slides/m7/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "CI vs. Laravel Form Workflow Map", "text": "CI vs. Laravel Form Workflow Map\nCodeIgniter pattern\n•\nShow form from controller\n•\nValidate submitted input\n•\nReturn errors to view\n•\nUpdate model record\nLaravel pattern\n•\nGET route + controller \nmethod\n•\n$request->validate([...])\n•\n@error and old() in Blade\n•\nEloquent update([...])", "image": "slides/m7/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Write the Update Method", "text": "Write the Update Method\nValidate first, then save the changes:\nTaskController.php\npublic function update(Request $request, Task $task)\n{\n$validated = $request->validate([\n'title' => ['required', 'string', 'max:100'],\n]);\n$task->update($validated);\nreturn redirect()->route('tasks.index');\n}", "image": "slides/m7/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Build the Edit Form", "text": "Build the Edit Form\nThe edit form posts to the update route and keeps the current \ntitle visible.\ntasks/edit.blade.php\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('tasks.update', $task) }}\">\n@csrf\n@method('PATCH')\n<input name=\"title\" value=\"{{ old('title', $task->title) }}\">\n<button type=\"submit\">Update</button>\n</form>", "image": "slides/m7/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "File Upload Form", "text": "File Upload Form\ntasks/edit.blade.php\n<form method=\"POST\"\naction=\"{{ route('tasks.upload', $task) }}\"\nenctype=\"multipart/form-data\">\n@csrf\n<label>Attachment</label>\n<input type=\"file\" name=\"attachment\">\n<button type=\"submit\">Upload</button>\n</form>\nThe action points to the upload route; enctype makes the browser send the file bytes.", "image": "slides/m7/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "Validate Uploaded File", "text": "Validate Uploaded File\nFile validation should restrict type and size before storage:\nTaskController.php\n$validated = $request->validate([\n'attachment' => [\n'nullable',\n'image',\n'mimes:jpg,jpeg,png',\n'max:2048',\n],\n]);\nmax:2048 means 2048 KB, or about 2 MB.", "image": "slides/m7/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Store the File on the Public Disk", "text": "Store the File on the Public Disk\nSave only the file path. The task record already exists, so \nupdate() adds the attachment field.\nTaskController.php\nif ($request->hasFile('attachment')) {\n$path = $request->file('attachment')\n->store('attachments', 'public');\n$task->update([\n'attachment' => $path,\n]);\n}", "image": "slides/m7/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "File Upload Commands", "text": "File Upload Commands\nphp artisan storage:link\n- Expose public storage files through /storage\n$request->hasFile('attachment')\n- Check that a file was submitted\nstore('attachments', 'public')\n- Save the uploaded file on the public disk\nStorage::disk('public')->delete($path)\n- Remove old files when replacing them", "image": "slides/m7/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Display the Uploaded File", "text": "Display the Uploaded File\nAfter storage:link, public disk files can be shown using the \n/storage path:\ntasks/show.blade.php\n@if ($task->attachment)\n<img\nsrc=\"{{ asset('storage/'.$task->attachment) }}\"\nalt=\"Task attachment\"\nwidth=\"120\"\n>\n@endif", "image": "slides/m7/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Replace an Existing Uploaded File", "text": "Replace an Existing Uploaded File\nWhen users upload a replacement, delete the old file after \nthe new file is accepted:\nTaskController.php\nif ($request->hasFile('attachment')) {\nif ($task->attachment) {\nStorage::disk('public')\n->delete($task->attachment);\n}\n$path = $request->file('attachment')\n->store('attachments', 'public');\n}", "image": "slides/m7/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Prepare Images for Display", "text": "Prepare Images for Display\nAfter validation, resize or crop the uploaded image before \nstoring the display path.\nTaskController.php\nuse Intervention\\Image\\Laravel\\Facades\\Image;\n$name = uniqid('avatar_') . '.jpg';\n$path = storage_path('app/public/attachments/' . $name);\nImage::read($request->file('attachment'))\n->cover(300, 300)\n->save($path);", "image": "slides/m7/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Form Request Classes Keep Controllers Clean", "text": "Form Request Classes Keep Controllers Clean\nFor longer validation rules, move the rules into a Form \nRequest class.\nTerminal\nphp artisan make:request UpdateTaskRequest\n# In the request class:\npublic function rules(): array\n{\nreturn [\n'title' => ['required', 'string', 'max:100'],\n];\n}", "image": "slides/m7/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Putting It Together: Edit Task", "text": "Putting It Together: Edit Task\nAfter validation, the controller \nupdates the record and returns \nto the list:\nTaskController.php\n$task->update($validated);\nreturn redirect()\n->route('tasks.index');\nlocalhost:8000/tasks/2/edit\nEdit Task\nTitle: Submit report\nUpdate", "image": "slides/m7/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Putting It Together: Upload Flow", "text": "Putting It Together: Upload Flow\nThe final upload method stores \nthe file, saves the path, and \nredirects.\nTaskController.php\n$path = $request->file('attachment')\n->store('attachments', 'public');\n$task->update([\n'attachment' => $path,\n]);\nreturn redirect()->route('tasks.show', $task);\nlocalhost:8000/tasks/3\nFix login bug\nUpload complete\n[ attachment preview ]", "image": "slides/m7/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Safe Upload Checklist", "text": "Safe Upload Checklist\nUse this order: validate, store, save the path, display safely, \nand clean up old files.\nSecurity habit\n$request->validate(['attachment' => [\n'nullable', 'image', 'max:2048'\n]]);\n// Do not trust the original filename.\n// Prepare image size before display.\n// Save only the generated path.\n// Delete old files only after replacement succeeds.", "image": "slides/m7/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nMissing @csrf\n•\nWrong form method or \naction\n•\nForgetting \n@method('PATCH')\n•\nNo enctype for file upload\n•\nSaving files before validation\nRemember\n•\nGET shows forms\n•\nPOST/PATCH submits data\n•\nvalidate() redirects with \nerrors\n•\nold() keeps user input\n•\nStorage manages uploaded \nfiles", "image": "slides/m7/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about Laravel forms, \nvalidation feedback, update workflows, \nor file uploads?", "image": "slides/m7/035.webp"}], "8": [{"page": 1, "title": "Web System Technologies", "text": "Web System Technologies\nIT0049", "image": "slides/m8/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "title": "MODULE 8", "text": "MODULE 8\nLaravel Full CRUD, Auth, and Email \nNotifications", "image": "slides/m8/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "title": "Objectives", "text": "Objectives\nBy the end of this session, you will be able to:\n•\nDevelop a complete Laravel CRUD workflow using \nresource routes\n•\nOrganize controller methods with a resource controller\n•\nApply guided authentication scaffolding and protected \nroutes\n•\nConfigure email settings for local testing\n•\nSend an email notification from a Laravel application \nevent", "image": "slides/m8/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "title": "What We'll Cover", "text": "What We'll Cover\nSubtopic 1\nFull CRUD and Resource \nControllers\n•\nResource routes\n•\nResource controller methods\n•\nRoute naming and CRUD flow\nSubtopic 2\nAuthentication\n•\nProtected routes\n•\nAccess checks\n•\nSubtopic 3: Email Notifications\n•\nSMTP settings\n•\nNotification triggers", "image": "slides/m8/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "title": "SUBTOPIC 1", "text": "SUBTOPIC 1\nFull CRUD and Resource Controllers", "image": "slides/m8/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "title": "Full CRUD Is a Complete Record Workflow", "text": "Full CRUD Is a Complete Record Workflow\n•\nCreate adds a new record.\n•\nRead lists records and shows one record.\n•\nUpdate edits an existing record.\n•\nDelete removes a record.\n•\nLaravel resource routes provide a standard URL and \nmethod pattern for these actions.", "image": "slides/m8/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "title": "Generate a Resource Controller", "text": "Generate a Resource Controller\n•\nArtisan can generate the \nstandard CRUD method \nnames for a controller. \nRegister the resource route \non the next slide before \nrunning route:list.\n$ php artisan \nmake:controller\nTaskController –resource\n--model=Task\nINFO  Controller created \nsuccessfully.", "image": "slides/m8/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "title": "Register One Resource Route", "text": "Register One Resource Route\nOne resource route registers the common CRUD routes:\nroutes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\TaskController;\nRoute::resource('tasks', TaskController::class);", "image": "slides/m8/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "title": "Resource Method Map", "text": "Resource Method Map\n•\nLaravel expects resource controllers to use standard \nmethod names.\n•\nThe names are predictable, so teams can read each \nother's controllers faster.", "image": "slides/m8/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "title": "The Resource Route Table", "text": "The Resource Route Table\nThe route table becomes the checklist for a full CRUD \nfeature:\n$ php artisan route:list --name=tasks\nGET       /tasks           tasks.index\nGET       /tasks/create    tasks.create\nPOST      /tasks           tasks.store\nGET       /tasks/{task}    tasks.show\nGET       /tasks/{task}/edit tasks.edit\nPUT/PATCH /tasks/{task}    tasks.update\nDELETE    /tasks/{task}    tasks.destroy", "image": "slides/m8/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "title": "The index method retrieves records and sends them to", "text": "The index method retrieves records and sends them to \nthe list view.\nIndex: Read Many Records\nTaskController.php\npublic function index()\n{\n$tasks = Task::latest()->get();\nreturn view('tasks.index', [\n'tasks' => $tasks,\n]);\n}", "image": "slides/m8/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "title": "Create and Store: Add a Record", "text": "Create and Store: Add a Record\n• create shows the form\n• store validates and saves the submitted data.", "image": "slides/m8/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "title": "The store method validates, creates, and redirects:", "text": "The store method validates, creates, and redirects:\nStore Method Example\n$validated = $request->validate([\n'title' => ['required', 'string', 'max:100'],\n]);\nTask::create($validated);\nreturn redirect()->route('tasks.index');", "image": "slides/m8/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "title": "Show and Edit: Work With One Record", "text": "Show and Edit: Work With One Record\n•\nshow displays one record.\n•\nedit displays the form for one existing record.\n•\nBoth methods can receive the Task model from the \nroute.\n•\nshow commonly returns tasks.show.\n•\nedit commonly returns tasks.edit.\n•\nBoth methods prepare one $task variable for Blade.", "image": "slides/m8/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "title": "Resource controllers make CRUD easier to", "text": "Resource controllers make CRUD easier to \nteach because every action has a predictable \nURL, route name, and method.", "image": "slides/m8/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "title": "SUBTOPIC 2", "text": "SUBTOPIC 2\nAuthentication", "image": "slides/m8/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "title": "Auth Checks the User", "text": "Auth Checks the User\nAuthentication checks who the user is.\n•\nAfter login, Laravel stores the user's authenticated \nstate in the session.\n•\nProtected routes require the user to be logged in \nbefore the page loads.\n•\nThe task manager should not allow guests to manage \nprivate records.", "image": "slides/m8/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "title": "Install the classroom auth scaffold, then run migrations and", "text": "Install the classroom auth scaffold, then run migrations and \nfrontend assets:\nInstall Guided Auth Scaffolding\n$ composer require laravel/breeze --dev\n$ php artisan breeze:install blade\n$ php artisan migrate\n$ npm install\n$ npm run dev", "image": "slides/m8/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "title": "Protect Resource Routes", "text": "Protect Resource Routes\nWrap the task routes in auth middleware:\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\nRoute::resource('tasks', TaskController::class);\n});\nRoute::get('/dashboard', function () {\nreturn view('dashboard');\n})->middleware('auth')->name('dashboard');\nGuests are redirected before protected pages.", "image": "slides/m8/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "title": "CI vs. Laravel Auth Map", "text": "CI vs. Laravel Auth Map\nCodeIgniter pattern\n•\nManual login controller\n•\nSession stores user data\n•\nController checks session\n•\nRedirect guests away\nLaravel pattern\n• Auth scaffolding routes\n• Session-backed web \nauth\n• auth middleware\n• Protected route groups", "image": "slides/m8/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "title": "Connect Records to the Logged-In User", "text": "Connect Records to the Logged-In User\nAfter auth, save through the user's tasks() relationship.\nTaskController.php\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n$validated = $request->validate([\n'title' => ['required', 'string', 'max:100'],\n]);\n$request->user()->tasks()->create($validated);\nreturn redirect()->route('tasks.index');\n}\nMigration: $table->foreignId('user_id')->constrained();\nUser.php: return $this->hasMany(Task::class);", "image": "slides/m8/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "title": "Filter Current User Records", "text": "Filter Current User Records\nThe index page should show only the current user's records:\nTaskController.php\npublic function index(Request $request)\n{\n$tasks = $request->user()\n->tasks()\n->latest()\n->get();\nreturn view('tasks.index', compact('tasks'));\n}\nBecause tasks() is defined on User, Laravel automatically filters by user_id.", "image": "slides/m8/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "title": "Protected Task Manager", "text": "Protected Task Manager\nThe same CRUD routes now require login:\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\nRoute::resource('tasks', TaskController::class);\n});\nThe same CRUD feature now has login protection.", "image": "slides/m8/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "title": "SUBTOPIC 3", "text": "SUBTOPIC 3\nEmail Notifications", "image": "slides/m8/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "title": "Email Notifications", "text": "Email Notifications\nConfigure mail in .env before sending messages:\n.env\nMAIL_MAILER=smtp\nMAIL_HOST=smtp.example.test\nMAIL_PORT=587\nMAIL_USERNAME=your_username\nMAIL_PASSWORD=your_password\nMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=\"noreply@example.test\"\nMAIL_FROM_NAME=\"${APP_NAME}\"\nUse test SMTP credentials only.", "image": "slides/m8/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "title": "Generate a Mailable Class", "text": "Generate a Mailable Class\nA mailable class describes the email that Laravel will send:\nTerminal\nphp artisan make:mail TaskCompletedMail --\nmarkdown=mail.tasks.completed\n# Created files:\napp/Mail/TaskCompletedMail.php\nresources/views/mail/tasks/completed.blade.php", "image": "slides/m8/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "title": "Email Commands", "text": "Email Commands\nphp artisan make:mail TaskCompletedMail\n - Create a mailable class\nMAIL_MAILER=smtp\n - Choose the mail transport\nMail::to(...)->send(...)\n - Send a mailable to one recipient\nphp artisan vendor:publish --tag=laravel-mail\n - Customize mail views when needed", "image": "slides/m8/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "title": "Write the Mailable Content", "text": "Write the Mailable Content\nThe mailable receives the task record and chooses a Blade \nemail view:\napp/Mail/TaskCompletedMail.php\nuse App\\Models\\Task;\nuse Illuminate\\Mail\\Mailable;\nuse Illuminate\\Mail\\Mailables\\Content;\nclass TaskCompletedMail extends Mailable\n{\npublic function __construct(public Task $task) {}\npublic function content(): Content\n{\nreturn new Content(markdown: 'mail.tasks.completed');\n}\n}", "image": "slides/m8/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "title": "Create the Email Blade View", "text": "Create the Email Blade View\nThe email body is still a Blade file, but it uses mail \ncomponents:\nresources/views/mail/tasks/completed.blade.php\n<x-mail::message>\n# Task Completed\nThe task **{{ $task->title }}** was marked complete.\n<x-mail::button :url=\"route('tasks.show', $task)\">\nView Task\n</x-mail::button>\n</x-mail::message>", "image": "slides/m8/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "title": "Send Email on Completion", "text": "Send Email on Completion\nSend after the update succeeds:\nTaskController.php\nuse App\\Mail\\TaskCompletedMail;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Mail;\nif ($task->status === 'done') {\nMail::to($request->user()->email)\n->send(new TaskCompletedMail($task));\n}", "image": "slides/m8/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "title": "Full CRUD in Action", "text": "Full CRUD in Action\nProtected routes show the \ncomplete task manager.\nroutes/web.php\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\nRoute::resource('tasks', TaskController::class);\n});\nlocalhost:8000/tasks\nTask Manager\nAdd Task\nEdit\nDelete\nLogout", "image": "slides/m8/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "title": "Putting It Together: Completion Email", "text": "Putting It Together: Completion Email\nWhen a task becomes complete, \nthe app sends a notification \nemail:\nTaskController.php\n$task->update($validated);\nif ($task->status === 'done') {\nMail::to($request->user()->email)\n->send(new TaskCompletedMail($task));\n}\nreturn back()->with('status', 'Status updated');\nlocalhost:8000/tasks/3/edit\nStatus updated\nEmail notification sent\nFix login bug", "image": "slides/m8/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "title": "Notification Design", "text": "Notification Design\nSend important emails once, keep sensitive data out, and \ntest with safe mail settings.\nNotification habit\nif ($task->wasChanged('status') && $task->status === 'done') {\n// Send one completion email.\n}\n// Avoid sending on page refresh.", "image": "slides/m8/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "title": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways", "text": "Common Mistakes & Key Takeaways\nWatch out for\n•\nManual routes duplicating \nresource routes\n•\nMissing auth middleware\n•\nShowing all users' records\n•\nReal SMTP secrets in \nscreenshots\n•\nEmails sent before update \nsucceeds\nRemember\n•\nRoute::resource maps \nCRUD\n•\nResource methods are \npredictable\n•\nauth protects pages\n•\n.env stores mail settings\n•\nTrigger emails responsibly", "image": "slides/m8/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "title": "Q&A SESSION", "text": "Q&A SESSION\nQuestions about resource CRUD, \nprotected routes, authentication \nscaffolding, or Laravel email \nnotifications?", "image": "slides/m8/035.webp"}]};
